#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d21652b-8d10-5c45-b44f-db44c924244e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257022} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad034e3-435a-54af-8b44-ed2fc0bf168f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257573} {:answer/id #uuid "863b68a3-5559-5883-8119-c9324587def7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257962} {:answer/id #uuid "a621cce8-352a-549d-ba06-938de99fba53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258228} {:answer/id #uuid "edd3b1c2-37e9-5a98-86ec-a93068bfe7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257573} :answer-field/id #uuid "27cdb6ef-9816-548d-bfcd-7bdee6ce6dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "530f83ac-873a-5207-ba3d-e9438dabfd29" :question/math-academy-id "q-124027" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={e}^{x}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,2)$ and $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {e}^{3+1}-0\\cdot {e}^{2+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c9589f-c628-5f4a-a78a-70eef556a95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69fec3-73fc-5815-8bc3-797953319185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} {:answer/id #uuid "8020ab34-3c45-5159-8e7f-6c5ce658702c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257922} {:answer/id #uuid "da2ca4d2-0f0c-5594-9b9e-6e91ba3983b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258687} {:answer/id #uuid "e253a7c1-631c-5b5e-ba82-916317b443b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c80ca6-5207-5a6c-a174-ad7e192a275e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388c57e6-e07f-5b15-a475-f9e96568702d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124028" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0794e38d-83c6-592a-8698-603e9bbcd5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256847} {:answer/id #uuid "239089df-2146-51b4-b115-a88f3d39c9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257080} {:answer/id #uuid "79dbb24e-2d02-5659-8575-c9dca1acb841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257858} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3ff693-01df-587b-b8f8-2302b9d2cfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258036} {:answer/id #uuid "bd179ab0-00b3-5bab-8759-a6525529b5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258432} :answer-field/id #uuid "7766e048-d9fa-5296-ac66-21288dbdd49b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e457dc8-8930-562f-905f-f48a2ebaba17" :question/math-academy-id "q-124029" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=x$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5570ad04-a911-54fe-882f-cae6a0eccb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16-{e}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257514} {:answer/id #uuid "69d52aa2-f5a3-518e-9e37-8487d0ca7564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257719} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdee35c-5f2c-5453-8ea0-524f7e2d5558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257879} {:answer/id #uuid "a641ae1f-fce3-54ae-b9d3-8c7c4dd0c256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+{e}^{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258233} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f247a0-2670-5ca0-8d3b-f9534636b266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257879} :answer-field/id #uuid "399493dc-69ea-5245-b7ef-1fc5d184a6d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33d927c3-e1a0-5a24-bd3b-65369e4fc02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124030" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\in [1,4],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "572cddd3-8f84-5b08-8f30-ba3696a9b1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257535} {:answer/id #uuid "980d5c51-5b40-5f56-bfb7-99d5b6db0c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258122} {:answer/id #uuid "ba90b885-3030-535b-a6d6-b20cfce89c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258416} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f40706-748a-5462-8a78-9770b7b5dbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258776} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fcb8ed-37e1-5237-bf6c-95c7528a9627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258887}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258416} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdaa45ba-790a-5b06-b275-79f77436acf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b4571c4-6fd7-5557-9855-d69404faa890" :question/math-academy-id "q-124032" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228757f4-13cc-55fe-9095-a48e100ceb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257067} {:answer/id #uuid "2a73a14f-a5c6-5495-a6bd-6839c844573f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257158} {:answer/id #uuid "75872a6e-2056-5f4d-a136-119cb8679c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257812} {:answer/id #uuid "c2078454-b9ed-517e-8d5c-46c93d61beb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258484} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbba96b-984e-5d92-80e9-dd02e8dff0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259001} :answer-field/id #uuid "db56c118-70b4-55be-8655-9ad9749d2c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cc34530a-2e34-53d7-8fcc-b9ff1814815f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124034" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b3eeba-4eac-5d44-9adc-3211c30eb7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257409} {:answer/id #uuid "5a534628-6707-5097-afac-65143ed14073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257569} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad476ab-38fd-5e4e-8d0a-5341b7b7b7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257574} {:answer/id #uuid "7dec0392-9c5a-5bf9-bc94-0852a51e8494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257896} {:answer/id #uuid "d412fdac-65be-56fb-9312-852052cc11df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257569} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ade967-0b40-518f-b3ab-2d00cfb750fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c2ccfca-31f7-5fd8-9901-48e158e0be78" :question/math-academy-id "q-124035" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40dc00cd-4c24-526f-b0be-714180e4f0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257359} {:answer/id #uuid "5febbd5f-4713-5fff-8f7f-17efac6589cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257622} {:answer/id #uuid "7696a3d2-8e7a-5c22-b23e-1b2752090aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257825} {:answer/id #uuid "ad788cdb-65a4-5dfe-965b-6ccf2a1c8f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258298} {:answer/id #uuid "e855ef6b-a065-56dc-b0dc-28e3c44034ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ebaf3f-afef-5801-a181-f3c0188294e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0853e6c9-d856-5aec-b1f2-e5d65484f5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124167" :question/problem "![](images/55/559a6b6cb717c10e60b77da402e310fc5895c814655b4ca1748eba294d712a32.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the upper half of the circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a half of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[(1)^{3}-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57205941-d594-5f13-9a16-fe0cd389d946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257534} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb566f1-93cb-5af8-a57a-1b33ba0d8412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257733} {:answer/id #uuid "74b195e5-6b2c-5425-b2b5-b8110e1a0731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257803} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c06057-adaf-5508-83fe-4fb2c2b5d5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258478} {:answer/id #uuid "e3249ef7-a202-5faa-95b6-80450e4666cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258478} :answer-field/id #uuid "124db33d-09bf-50c0-a6e0-28e8a683c661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "105c34ef-d15b-56de-a1a8-f1b42bfb4fee" :question/math-academy-id "q-124173" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbd3fdd67ac209544717fc7d4b33831c7cdfd9f6839a7c1410f43ffd385d63ff.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(0,-1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021c19b6-a87f-59bb-986b-654c56b918a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256802} {:answer/id #uuid "54fdfd50-de07-5cfb-b6d6-270b66947185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257507} {:answer/id #uuid "6abe279c-1db9-56ef-99fe-6e272def1150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723} {:answer/id #uuid "debdbff0-5139-5a2f-ac8e-84956824c0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258722} {:answer/id #uuid "f66ec332-aeec-5f7f-8bb0-93630c7f3749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723} :answer-field/id #uuid "4358961a-328e-53e0-ad60-06b7463ba396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd888ac9-9398-5cd7-abd9-7da37aaa7d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-124175" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(\\sqrt{x-1}+y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}-t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ceacbe-c767-5fbf-a196-9dc0a6e3ef27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256975} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2a09aa-6780-57b9-84e1-0804ea1c0959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257023} {:answer/id #uuid "37384247-4488-580e-ab77-fc38b0f10247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257266} {:answer/id #uuid "3999c92c-0ae4-51a9-9073-6aeee6da3e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257290} {:answer/id #uuid "7eedef54-bfcb-5d25-a867-579313c708bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea48476d-dc43-5cf6-bf5c-5a8d7d48b4f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f008788-4322-589f-b4f5-5bd6b468b9b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124177" :question/problem "![](images/27/272b0afabca579f65cbaa7949fd1be70427ff0fd860eb953207bbc8eda7b6e9c.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{3x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-left quarter of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-9[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb77b4b-9f0a-5ae7-b350-e9c8ee7ac7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257019} {:answer/id #uuid "50f9dd65-239e-59ad-8a3d-93b2debc6b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257473} {:answer/id #uuid "6b988f18-d213-513d-9832-3e63e11d42ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257732} {:answer/id #uuid "bf507ac3-4f74-5b49-a090-ea632c0a57d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450} {:answer/id #uuid "f63874ba-42bf-5ec0-a115-516773880a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4362a76-2e86-5ca7-8502-9a0cc1964b8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cdf631e5-2841-5a67-a8ff-d0eebc8b7e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-124212" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfa95100566fce9ab113b00ae53d1a76e8977b68aa4bac0e9b6892a9930b70d4.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x}\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the lower half of the circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f7b9b1-6205-5e9a-abd6-d1dfcd93b57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256979} {:answer/id #uuid "614bd43e-4103-5afd-b258-686e690c6a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257637} {:answer/id #uuid "ac79318b-520f-5618-92c3-a94976c39773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258284} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e76e62-2425-5791-b451-5fd5063d747e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258479} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5e0345-52da-5c07-878f-0a4c916fd920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258996} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e95fb8c-bf91-5786-bbd2-5640b44aefed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1879edf5-1344-5830-a3f2-7e9af982bae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124217" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb4c4828626255e26e298a0cdc202b37852df9d39bcfe15e0e67d350d90f774a.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(2-2)),\\,(0+t(2-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)+{x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (2t)}{2}\\right)+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+4)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4+\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2[4-(-1)+1] \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01825306-ac24-5300-973f-a0c4b791365b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256796} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27a531-9de1-56f9-836a-e1736077898d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257349} {:answer/id #uuid "8e35d176-d483-5c6c-8219-0e38dad049fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258035} {:answer/id #uuid "9c536383-f7c3-5cfe-8d3b-1ffc7943f5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258157} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d64c61-750a-55b2-80ed-e2d9402be4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258035} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae531638-bc65-5f25-9c4c-6658f8c3500e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "591574ce-dde0-5375-8d9f-24f9b5f86769" :question/math-academy-id "q-124223" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01c4633489a913d93f86ccc83580963ea5cd41bed31347108730a7d60c3ebf.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3,$ traversed from the point $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $(0,-\\sqrt{3}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2168c8cd-5aeb-586f-8d06-cc8367809f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257056} {:answer/id #uuid "2b650bd1-0837-5f08-99d5-a807e4c27e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257170} {:answer/id #uuid "35123647-df55-5d75-89f2-0aca23bfb5d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257249} {:answer/id #uuid "59c84fb3-3212-58ee-92d9-6c0807dbf87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257560} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2b2604-95aa-59ca-8d59-13a86f0b5357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257560} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b84b78-85d8-5936-a4d9-bebaf84cdf71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a31726b8-8b33-50ff-9a55-ad044b065273" :question/math-academy-id "q-124234" :question/problem "![](images/43/43c8d1ced440e0fe79052c1329ff3af865e1fdbfb4cc0c7ad0a9c176592358e9.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(1+t(2-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)+(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3t-1)+({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cb2f45-a0a2-5f1e-bf8e-2270b4bdb803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{t}^{2}-t+7)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257213} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4bbd3a-3496-5b69-bcf9-e8a2d9e5408a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(18{t}^{2}+t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257577} {:answer/id #uuid "66a80b9e-0979-58cf-8713-06c17381084c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257688} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0a3d3c-41b5-5b9c-8564-d3b85a1aaff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{t}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257751} {:answer/id #uuid "84976c13-5b58-592e-9b5b-401ce06fe5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{t}^{2}+7t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257688} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8fa6e0a-e87c-5a7d-8db9-0d8ef84a9586" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0410ccfc-9f5a-5741-9bf7-f1f601669aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-124270" :question/problem "![](images/58/58995f1bc5b45dbe0d30a9d0cbf704d792b955e7195c0e4dc3610ddf2e576579.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,2t-1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(-1+t(1-(-1)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,2t-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)^{2}-(2t-1) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-6t+1-2t+1 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-8t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57321071-ed92-5969-b645-38b2e65f944b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257537} {:answer/id #uuid "62251978-7b64-514f-956c-3adbb9ea5ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257647} {:answer/id #uuid "6c038848-0fc0-55d3-b62d-414a628f5e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(e+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257735} {:answer/id #uuid "707e588a-fbf2-5a85-9ad2-4cf1abd166f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257768} {:answer/id #uuid "de648ba3-07ad-5cb0-a4a0-5469d13b5ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257647} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae7cf10-da4c-5368-b5cf-081f5c11280f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "11aa222d-e9ea-5ecd-ab97-032f5281bcab" :question/math-academy-id "q-124273" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e5257867dae67451500793687a2da24ee31a73a17c691d8d8a6dcded46e8993.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the three-quarters of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={cos}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275d71c4-46a9-5261-9eb4-5c0c8e6b9dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257127} {:answer/id #uuid "6e447aa7-08e1-50c3-91b6-e0971243693d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257753} {:answer/id #uuid "88cbd20a-ccc7-55e9-8402-c84e72ce8a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257982} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dc8a41-c7c1-5d26-b4f6-2e6bbd735d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258227} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e80ec8-d12c-5842-b5d1-daee11dbe703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257753} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d13a439-5bf8-5f5d-9ec6-8ae8c6bdbf6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7eef0fe8-9d36-5fca-a5de-d79c02a74295" :question/math-academy-id "q-124279" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d55ece1948f9ecb7da7d489b86a297651a34f409f83ba2470f6dd06678a375e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f83140-8d60-5e3b-8557-38a73fea7b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256790} {:answer/id #uuid "130cd1bb-c702-58af-b317-816f00770ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256955} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8051c9-5f19-5e7c-9a70-1cd1c77f1c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257745} {:answer/id #uuid "933f36c7-2d77-5384-a819-869eaeb53ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258081} {:answer/id #uuid "b62bc8f9-e700-500d-bcd6-4f8a56698270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257745} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e765f7a-7a63-5189-8586-c70b56370d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "76e58dcc-8bc1-5f3d-ae09-2c38a29543eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124287" :question/problem "![](images/69/69a10b5524670d31cfc9f2982e37d58a5ee9ca39fe49f35b9dace011275a635a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(9-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along three-quarters of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(1,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =9(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271f180d-3faa-5476-a054-bf22f99176bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257122} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6abd70-33c0-5278-82e7-159c499aad7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257447} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef47045-065b-557c-a1cb-f734f625b80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x{e}^{-x}-3{e}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258180} {:answer/id #uuid "b34f8ff6-8e7b-5a4a-b1de-16e75dced547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-x}-3x{e}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258342} {:answer/id #uuid "d29bd4f3-02bb-5eb5-b6ba-5b257eab8b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258180} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fd06f4-4187-5190-9ee6-b13410e9fa3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a4ea9ed-1853-52ed-ab6f-46794d76e304" :question/math-academy-id "q-12462" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3x{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 3x{e}^{-x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot (-{e}^{-x})-\\int (-{e}^{-x})\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3x{e}^{-x}+3\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-x}-3{e}^{-x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2061316d-1856-583b-a012-175c95e1c965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257043} {:answer/id #uuid "563f3b75-047f-5df7-8de7-9221c43542f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257524} {:answer/id #uuid "721d5e3a-d79f-55f9-9018-78465894af00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257783} {:answer/id #uuid "78eb821b-6c70-554e-972c-afc5237e47d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257851} {:answer/id #uuid "a6fda23d-0a63-5c16-8aa0-61bd7f16d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258240} :answer-field/id #uuid "b256fecd-a217-5f81-b776-bf372703f7c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9d024fb-1731-5e0e-8fd1-3a79afb84b21" :question/math-academy-id "q-124652" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(-1,2)$ to the point $B(1,0),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(i-(-i+2\\,j)) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(2\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2-2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)(2)+(-2)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)-1\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{t}^{2}+6t-3\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}-3t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}+3-3]-2[0] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f4f537-9c38-5a4a-92d9-1fc05c60f90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256834} {:answer/id #uuid "3789ffb2-b7e7-5c19-9e46-46878e9dd27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257270} {:answer/id #uuid "78bd4c3d-cccd-5c94-8184-aae6764245be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257849} {:answer/id #uuid "df481504-80af-5bb2-8167-dfe689532510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t(1-4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258727} {:answer/id #uuid "e25e2064-e1d7-50b0-b597-8c407a11946b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258751} :answer-field/id #uuid "046799fa-5877-570c-92f0-0954916f5fcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1324f71e-2d99-5801-9cdc-c3861396a17d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124656" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77262323-79ed-5ade-a88d-cfe620b7da6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257835} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f4a4ec-6fb3-5b3a-ace0-98c820d9456a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258256} {:answer/id #uuid "ce873cc3-03d3-531f-9ee9-9641247e9f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258576} {:answer/id #uuid "da94ceb2-a1b8-5f7d-817e-d3c142cc2c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258692} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7f1f33-0822-5dd8-b24e-299b64b487d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-2\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258256} :answer-field/id #uuid "665ec369-2b3a-59ec-a54c-232cc0215c3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b45898cb-232a-5e7b-9fc7-75fcf27a9882" :question/math-academy-id "q-124657" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "518dc48b-166a-5cab-b682-ceb513ca018f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{4}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257475} {:answer/id #uuid "5514bb7f-2242-59f6-a527-7a456fa434c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257508} {:answer/id #uuid "72ddc658-03d6-5c6d-9682-1d117f5448cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6}-3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257792} {:answer/id #uuid "d551764d-1496-5882-b891-2f87604939f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{6}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258650} {:answer/id #uuid "ef05dfcc-4837-55ea-a44c-b1bc4977ee48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258854} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac77208e-6c5b-5fce-9439-9602678d59af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d143a12d-610a-5ab9-bde5-0709d25859cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124658" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(4,2),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0\\,i+0\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j+t(4\\,i+2\\,j-(0\\,i+0\\,j)) \\\\  & =4t\\,i+2t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=4t$ and $y(t)=2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=4\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t+2t}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6t}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6(1)}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{6(0)}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f6d91d8-d6dd-52c9-b3ff-b1db44329a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257620} {:answer/id #uuid "6266e64e-03c1-58e9-a2a7-faa1e9d1ab4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257649} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e0d134-f8c3-5cad-825a-eca5a50c39ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258774} {:answer/id #uuid "eef0270e-6352-5435-b70f-a6965924b8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi -5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258853} {:answer/id #uuid "fddbbdf7-66ee-5acd-97fd-6d8474436d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c70434-b863-58cf-ba58-7f3a3ae42742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3189421a-c22a-5fa2-87db-a7498ce87212" :question/math-academy-id "q-124661" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }((x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+6t]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+6\\pi )-(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3\\pi )] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}+6\\pi )-(\\frac{5}{4}+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08792d36-b712-50c1-b364-d2539ef8bc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256854} {:answer/id #uuid "10c49561-a693-5a9e-8fe4-f237ac8c02dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256937} {:answer/id #uuid "26adfe2c-c9e9-5b3f-a5e0-1481d5b90994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257116} {:answer/id #uuid "6c787fa5-b320-5415-99de-06c8e742d4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257739} {:answer/id #uuid "6e385de7-39aa-5450-a0e9-ee6c4afd30d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256854} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed846ac5-800c-5ebb-aa61-1cb4d2210b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "06d9e7d7-47d1-54b7-9b9c-2dd2d48786ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-124663" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,1)$ to the point $N(1,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+j+t(i+3\\,j-(-i+j)) \\\\  & =-i+j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)^{2}\\cdot 2+(2t-1)^{2}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t+1)+(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}8{t}^{2}+2\\,dt \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{4}{3}+10]-4[0] \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fcc3d9-e48a-542e-9d30-6b397fb1a498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256961} {:answer/id #uuid "1869c282-abb4-524c-be28-650f45a1ebb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256994} {:answer/id #uuid "3697d3f0-d2a9-5157-9c3a-73b0ea60e2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257260} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0cfb60-19ef-5381-83bb-81e635e37ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257429} {:answer/id #uuid "b332b886-e893-52d6-b7df-0c964a1cd0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256994} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f64dde8-4609-5f96-b95a-2b0b8b027fbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bbd34e81-f803-5fbc-b7c5-b362f720fe60" :question/math-academy-id "q-124664" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $P(1,2)$ to point $Q(4,4),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}((x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t({t}^{2}-2t)+({t}^{2}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2[(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+{2}^{2})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+{1}^{2})] \\\\  & =2[4-\\frac{8}{3}+4-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}-1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{53}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c3f67e-c143-52cf-8d04-0883a4d5a256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256943} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfb3475-8c2e-59e8-84f2-1ebef1519964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257605} {:answer/id #uuid "79b7785e-2e75-5831-91d7-a32ea05f26cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257857} {:answer/id #uuid "a627c339-0874-52fc-ab69-4b8d01f804f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258229} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c4c7b4-80c1-5190-8730-555b2288af36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258910}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258229} :answer-field/id #uuid "39dbfdbb-3be4-5981-9a19-60472431c20b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e2357f5-0311-5b8a-87b7-eaae763c04c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124665" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6t]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+9\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +6\\pi ) \\\\  & =(0+9\\pi )-(-1+6\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi +1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181e2f70-d760-5c6f-a30f-585047479128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256990} {:answer/id #uuid "381edb78-eaaa-5872-8c1d-fd2478651f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257275} {:answer/id #uuid "55d0b7e0-71bf-594a-a93e-ca2764905f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257521} {:answer/id #uuid "71b1c76c-c17a-5ae5-a386-f4803282a9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257780} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9bb5b2-b51a-5f5b-9ec6-58471882be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257521} :answer-field/id #uuid "039596a1-9fe5-54ad-8161-f20c3677fd50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "170a6ced-a411-5130-91d2-063d0a1b4a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-124668" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{3}$ traversed from the point $P(1,1)$ to point $Q(2,8),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+3t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})}+3(2)]-[-\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}+3(1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+6+\\frac{1}{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecde6e98-53fd-5ddd-bdc1-6ff205d00d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462139} {:answer/id #uuid "f49d1e0e-f360-53d6-9140-755bc53425ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462140} :answer-field/id #uuid "a82752cf-bf54-5079-8c9f-a8fa8464cd75" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462823} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17fa137d-526a-57e9-8c8d-806b8a6e7139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462141} {:answer/id #uuid "482d1113-1761-5039-8696-fb7a9c674eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462141} :answer-field/id #uuid "62c5e294-13bc-5717-9f9c-3fef7f5d4399" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6953b99-2176-55bb-9f0e-69ab32d7d93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462143} {:answer/id #uuid "b13b7134-2fe3-59e1-90c8-cc035011e284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462143} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df54704-5657-56ec-913b-dbeb0cf13cfe" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+x\\ne 0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "387d1688-e72e-5fdc-bd02-55d9c19c099b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124775" :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge x.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes,\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole (containing all points of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$).\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+x\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=-x.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ only."}]}
